# Rust lineage 2 — initial v1 submission

## Delivery and operation

The workspace contains executable `build.sh`, the Linux AArch64 ELF `service`,
Rust source in `src/`, pinned `Cargo.toml`/`Cargo.lock`, tests in `tests/`, and
`EXPERIMENTS.md`. No ABI v2 or group commit is implemented.

Build and test only inside the supplied Linux broker:

```sh
python3 run.py './build.sh' 300
python3 run.py './tests/run.sh' 300
```

Launch: `service DATADIR SOCKET_PATH`. The socket path must be fresh. RocksDB
holds its native exclusive writer lock. Shutdown drains all accepted persistent
work and admitted evaluations, flushes queued socket responses where possible,
joins workers, closes the database and exits zero.

## Design and durable transitions

- `main.rs`: single coordinator, four encoding worker threads, normal FIFO
  dispatch. Workers can finish out of order; the coordinator commits at most the
  next contiguous admission sequence in each loop. Evaluations use the same four
  slots and have no persistent rows.
- `state.rs`: exact schema-1 keys from the wire/storage contract. Admission writes
  one pending job, its ID index, sequence and accounting in one synchronous
  WriteBatch. Each terminal batch writes one receipt, deletes pending/cancel
  keys, releases accounting and advances the checkpoint. ID indexes remain.
  First cancellation writes its intent synchronously before response; repeated
  pending cancellation is idempotent. Running cancellation waits for the native
  call to return before terminalizing and releasing payload accounting.
- `native.rs`: `repr(C)` ABI v1 only. A worker creates the native arena and derives
  item pointers from `rn_arena_data`; it owns and destroys the arena after verify
  returns. The Rust input is borrowed throughout. No raw native pointers cross
  threads and no unsafe Send/Sync implementations were added.
- `transport.rs`: up to eight clients, separate reader/writer threads, bounded
  40 MiB framing. Each reader waits until its response is written before advancing
  to the next request. A 32-message/80 MiB output queue reserves response space;
  at most 30 terminal events are outstanding per connection. Events may be
  dropped. Socket writes time out after two seconds; a disconnected or stalled
  client never cancels its jobs. Admission response is enqueued before any
  terminal event for that admission.
- `wire.rs` and `sha256.rs`: strict IDs, lowercase even-length hex, mode/operation
  enums, batch byte/count limits, decimal-string output, and generic SHA-256
  identity hashing. Transaction parsing, resource checks and transaction hashes
  are exclusively delegated to the supplied adapter.
- `storage.rs`: supplied dynamic RocksDB wrapper extended with iterator support
  and fatal write-error handling. Compression off, 128 MiB cache, 64 MiB write
  buffer, two write buffers, two background jobs, sync=true, WAL enabled.

Cancellation and terminal writes are serialized by the coordinator. No other
operation completes between terminal disposition selection and C write entry.
A cancellation already acknowledged must therefore be reflected in that receipt;
a cancellation overlapping the write waits and observes too_late afterward.
No service barrier or scheduling test hooks were added.

Recovery checks the whole keyspace before opening the listener: schema version,
required counters, contiguous pending/receipt partition, ID-index bijection,
cancellation references, payload byte totals and recomputed pending digests.
Recovered cancellations remain cancelled; other pending v1 jobs resume at normal
priority. Existing terminal receipts are retained, not rebuilt.

## Failure handling

An arena allocation failure returns execution_failure for evaluate. For an
acknowledged job it stops the service with diagnostics and leaves durable pending
state for restart. A returned RocksDB write error prints a diagnostic and exits
immediately without acknowledging the transition or attempting another write.

The pinned RocksDB build may itself assert under a kernel file-size limit before
returning an error to Rust. The test observed native assertion diagnostics and
nonzero exit, no admission acknowledgment, intact recovered rows and successful
restart. This is recorded separately from the service's returned-error path.

## Validation evidence

- Release build succeeds with the original pinned dependency lockfile.
- Three Rust unit tests pass: ABI layout, JSON-line framing, and standard SHA-256
  vectors including one million bytes and incremental chunking.
- Seventeen independent integration tests pass. These cover native adapter
  parity, exact/prefix and malformed inputs, 4 MiB and 4,096-item native
  boundaries, request and batch limits, digest padding, retry conflicts,
  concurrent identical admission, eight-client execution, client cap, response
  and notification ordering, disconnects, slow readers, partial input, draining,
  exclusive locking, schema rejection, full 1,024-job/64 MiB budgets, cancellation
  intent recovery, live running cancellation, repeated kill/restart, and native
  storage failure. Inspection uses the RocksDB C API only after the writer exits.
- `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings` passes. The supplied public smoke
  check passes; it is not durability qualification.

Tests include externally seeded legal schema snapshots for deterministic full
queues and cancellation recovery. No evaluator was inspected or invoked. No
network, external source, Bitcoin library, other workspace or agent was used.

Not demonstrated: native ASan, whole-service race/TSan coverage, power-loss
durability, forced arena-allocation failure, or the campaign's external
call-entry/barrier instrumentation. Do not infer those qualifications from these
tests. The native-failure test can abort before exercising returned-error code.
No performance conclusion is claimed.

## Interpretation and next phase

- Unknown wire fields are ignored as reserved; malformed known fields are
  rejected. Explicit normal priority is tolerated; high/other priority is
  rejected in v1. Do not treat this as v2 support.
- Terminal cancellation, including an already-cancelled receipt, returns
  too_late; repeated cancellation while pending returns ok.
- Oversized/invalid adapter batch requests are invalid_request without writes.
  Per-transaction byte/item resource limits remain adapter result statuses.
- An identical existing submission can return existing during drain; new IDs
  and evaluations return draining.
- The supplied contracts refer to a campaign freeze that is not present in this
  workspace. Implementation was explicitly authorized; campaign gate evidence
  is not claimed. No predecessor handoff was present.

Preserve v1 disk receipts, cancellations and inputs when doing later maintenance.
ABI v2 offsets/bitmap and priority scheduling are intentionally absent. Group
commit belongs only to the optimization round and must preserve the same native
write-entry cancellation boundary and durability.

Initial implementation took approximately 22 minutes, within the 90-minute cap;
reading was completed early in the preceding phase. Token usage and monetary
cost are not available from this workspace. See `EXPERIMENTS.md` for failed
attempts and their disposition.
